# The dataset is content-addressed: manifest.json records a sha256 and a byte count
# for every file, so a checkout that rewrites line endings changes the corpus without
# changing a line of engine code. LF in the working tree is therefore not a style
# preference; it is what the hashes describe.
#
# Two opposite mistakes break that, and the hash check cannot tell them apart on its
# own: a checkout with core.autocrlf=true turning the committed LF into CRLF (Windows
# alone), and a generator writing CRLF in the first place, which records a hash of
# bytes git never stores here (any platform -- both generators once did this, and only
# a clean checkout caught it). tools/check_dataset.py now distinguishes the two and
# asserts that every corpus file is LF-only, so the second mistake fails at build
# time instead of on someone else's machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Not text: git must not guess, and a wrong guess corrupts them.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
